            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1233-f2" n="35" xml:id="d1233-f2-content">
                <p>Note No. 3088; see footnote 32, <ref target="#pg1269">p. 1269</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1233-f3" n="36" xml:id="d1233-f3-content">
                <p>Note No. 563247; for translation, see <hi rendition="#italic">U.S. Railway
                        Mission in Mexico, 1942–1946</hi>, p. 92.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1233-f4" n="37" xml:id="d1233-f4-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1233-f5" n="38" xml:id="d1233-f5-content">
                <p>The Sureste Railway, the connection with Guatemala and the south, had been
                    severely damaged by heavy rains and floods.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
